WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.067 --> 00:00:02.250
There's a scene in 2019's "Little Women"

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.700
where Saoirse Ronan and
Timothée Chalamet dance

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:07.100
outside giant windows
that look into a party

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.010
where everyone else is dancing.

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:11.090
In the 1994 adaptation,

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:13.080
this scene is filmed with Jo and Laurie

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:16.370
dancing in a study,
dining room, and hallway.

00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:18.350
Both sequences establish Jo and Laurie

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:19.990
as outsiders in society,

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:22.690
but what separates the 1994 film

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:24.610
from the 2019 adaptation

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:25.870
is the staging.

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:28.830
The 1994 film uses four set pieces

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:31.030
while Gerwig uses only one,

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:33.637
which more effectively
positions Jo and Laurie

00:00:33.637 --> 00:00:35.160
as outsiders.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.882
Where Gerwig stages a
scene is just one way

00:00:37.882 --> 00:00:39.630
she illustrates her genius.

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:43.090
And it's one of the three
reasons she deserved to win,

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:44.790
or at least get nominated for,

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:46.631
best director at the Oscars.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.020
We all felt it coming.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:55.780
In several award shows
leading up to the Oscars,

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.240
women were left out,

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:58.940
including former Oscar nominee

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.600
and supposed shoo-in Greta Gerwig.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.005
But as Gerwig writes in her screenplay,

00:01:04.005 --> 00:01:06.830
"What women are allowed into
the club of geniuses anyway?"

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:10.067
Jo: And I'm so sick of people saying that

00:01:10.067 --> 00:01:12.333
love is just all a woman is fit for.

00:01:12.333 --> 00:01:13.550
I'm so sick of it!

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:16.310
Narrator: Let's start with
Gerwig's brilliant blocking.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:18.232
A hallmark of a good scene is when

00:01:18.232 --> 00:01:20.537
everyone in that scene
has something to do.

00:01:20.537 --> 00:01:23.930
Gerwig takes this concept to
the next level with movement.

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:26.730
Characters effortlessly
move in and out of focus

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:29.630
in their scenes at
precisely the right time.

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:31.500
To illustrate this, we can take a look

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.603
at the scene where the Marches return home

00:01:33.603 --> 00:01:36.030
from giving the Hummels
their Christmas breakfast.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:37.730
Meg: Grandfather?
Hannah: Yes.

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:38.698
Meg: Why?

00:01:38.698 --> 00:01:40.560
Hannah: He saw you giving
your Christmas breakfast away,

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.500
and he wanted you to enjoy the day.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:44.000
Amy: But I thought he was a mean old man.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.440
Marmee: That's so generous of him.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.800
Jo: His grandson Laurie
put the idea into his head.

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:48.810
I know he did.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:50.650
We should make friends with him.

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:52.600
Narrator: Now let's break this down.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.040
All the Marches come through the door.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.090
Amy notices the spread first,

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.600
and everybody follows
her around the table.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:01.560
We learn that she likes nice things

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:04.270
and that's she's aware of material needs.

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:07.480
Next, Jo leads the group to the window

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:10.440
when she hears that the spread
came from the Laurences.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.350
She's more idealistic.

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:15.060
Marmee stays planted at
the center of the room

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.390
because she's the anchor of the family.

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:18.731
Amy leads the group back to

00:02:18.731 --> 00:02:20.950
what she cares about: the feast.

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:22.533
With the movement in this scene,

00:02:22.533 --> 00:02:23.841
Gerwig uses the space

00:02:23.841 --> 00:02:26.170
to let her characters organically move.

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:28.111
Their familiarity makes it feel

00:02:28.111 --> 00:02:29.569
like they really live there.

00:02:29.569 --> 00:02:32.863
But the choreography also
informs character dynamics

00:02:32.863 --> 00:02:34.440
that are important to the story.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.210
Jo and Amy tend to lead.

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:37.850
Beth and Meg tend to follow.

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:40.290
Marmee holds everyone together.

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:43.090
Another great example
of movement and blocking

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:46.530
is in this scene between Meryl
Streep and Saoirse Ronan.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:48.980
Jo and Aunt March are dramatic foils.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:50.770
Where Jo is always moving,

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:53.950
Aunt March is almost always still.

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:56.040
Jo has gone off to look out the window.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.580
With this decision,
Gerwig points out again

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:01.390
she's looking out for possibilities.

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:04.800
Aunt March: Is there a reason
you stopped reading Belsham?

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.940
Narrator: Aunt March forces
her back down to continue

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:11.000
reading, reflecting her desire
to get Jo back to earth.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.030
Aunt March: You'll wish
you had behaved better.

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:15.237
Narrator: The ensuing
comedic exchange shows that

00:03:15.237 --> 00:03:19.100
Jo does not do well with
being forced to sit still.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:20.841
Aunt March: [chuckling] Oh, well.

00:03:20.841 --> 00:03:23.408
No, no one makes their own way.

00:03:23.408 --> 00:03:24.933
Not really.

00:03:24.933 --> 00:03:26.100
Least of all a woman.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:27.904
You'll need to marry well.

00:03:27.904 --> 00:03:29.080
Jo: But you are not married, Aunt March.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:30.470
Aunt March: Well, that's because I'm rich.

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:32.011
Narrator: Character-driven blocking

00:03:32.011 --> 00:03:33.520
expertly executed.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:34.640
Let's talk about pace.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:37.080
Emma Watson describes the
dialogue in "Little Women"

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.811
in an interview with Sony Pictures

00:03:38.811 --> 00:03:41.360
as having dashes at the end of each line.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:43.675
This means that the lines run together.

00:03:43.675 --> 00:03:45.633
Greta Gerwig: But it is in a rhythm.

00:03:45.633 --> 00:03:47.658
It's not in a random rhythm.

00:03:47.658 --> 00:03:49.795
Gerwig describes the effect created

00:03:49.795 --> 00:03:51.290
as "choreographed chaos"

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:53.820
and a "cacophony of words and sounds."

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:56.090
[actors talking over each other]

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:56.923
[sharp gasp]

00:03:57.906 --> 00:03:59.351
[Amy gasps]

00:03:59.351 --> 00:04:00.960
Now, you might be thinking,

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:03.201
"Wait, if that's the way
the lines were written,

00:04:03.201 --> 00:04:05.367
doesn't that illustrate
why it's a great screenplay

00:04:05.367 --> 00:04:07.730
instead of why it's excellent directing?"

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:08.950
It's a great screenplay,

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:11.410
but delivery and pace are a collaboration

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:13.180
between director and performer.

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:15.620
Where exactly does this
character interrupt the other?

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:17.226
Jo: It would be a disaster if we married.

00:04:17.226 --> 00:04:18.059
Laurie: It wouldn't be a disaster.

00:04:18.059 --> 00:04:18.975
Jo: OK, we'd be miserable!

00:04:18.975 --> 00:04:19.808
Laurie: Jo...

00:04:19.808 --> 00:04:21.082
Narrator: These are
decisions Gerwig was making

00:04:21.082 --> 00:04:23.740
in rehearsal and on set as the director.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:26.840
She knew exactly how she
wanted the dialogue to sound,

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:28.180
taking the words she had written

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:29.870
and bringing them to life.

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:31.705
Let's take another look
at the Christmas scene

00:04:31.705 --> 00:04:33.530
to see this in action.

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:36.034
The scene starts with a very fast rhythm,

00:04:36.034 --> 00:04:38.621
but then it slows to a
perfectly engineered pace

00:04:38.621 --> 00:04:39.496
for us to learn about

00:04:39.496 --> 00:04:42.229
the character dynamics and relationships.

00:04:42.229 --> 00:04:44.540
Jo: His grandson Laurie
put the idea into his head.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:45.530
I know he did.

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:46.570
We should make friends with him.

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:47.820
Narrator: Jo admires Laurie

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.340
and wants to be friends.

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:50.840
Marmee: He's a very kind man

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:53.672
who lost his little girl
when she was only a child,

00:04:53.672 --> 00:04:54.800
and now his son as well.

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:55.633
Beth: His daughter died?

00:04:55.633 --> 00:04:57.930
Narrator: Mr. Laurence
has a heart of gold.

00:04:57.930 --> 00:04:59.917
Amy: But doesn't Laurie
just seem so romantic?

00:04:59.917 --> 00:05:00.870
He's half Italian.

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:02.800
Narrator: Amy sees Laurie romantically.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:05.500
What could be seen as
purely expositional dialogue

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:08.350
is transformed to
natural family discussion

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:10.234
that anybody could see themselves having

00:05:10.234 --> 00:05:11.940
around a dinner table.

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:14.643
All that in a scene that's
less than a minute long.

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:19.388
Here's an example of a scene
that tries this technique,

00:05:19.388 --> 00:05:22.138
but not to the same level of success.

00:05:22.138 --> 00:05:23.710
Randy: I just don't...please.

00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:25.870
Rick: Look, Randy, I'm asking
you to help me out, man.

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:27.063
If the answer's no,

00:05:27.063 --> 00:05:30.210
the answer's no, not no with excuses.

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:31.943
Narrator: Rick and Randy
overstep each other

00:05:31.943 --> 00:05:33.526
in this conversation,

00:05:33.526 --> 00:05:35.810
but why doesn't it feel as natural?

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:37.510
It's the movement and the pace.

00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:40.170
Most of this scene plays
out in a static wide shot,

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:42.620
but instead of lending
itself to the tightness

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:45.710
of the trailer, the superwide
lens gives both characters

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:48.350
more space that they don't really utilize.

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:50.400
They both stay planted on either side,

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:52.700
never breaking the famous 180-degree line

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:54.480
and playing it very safe.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:56.578
Leonardo DiCaprio makes good choices

00:05:56.578 --> 00:05:57.970
to get ready through the scene,

00:05:57.970 --> 00:06:00.461
but Kurt Russell doesn't have much to do.

00:06:00.461 --> 00:06:02.930
The pacing of the interruptions
feels choreographed

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:04.728
rather than each actor trying to get

00:06:04.728 --> 00:06:06.533
the upper hand in the conversation.

00:06:07.806 --> 00:06:10.760
Gerwig closely collaborated
with all creative departments

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:13.370
to create the perfect
staging for her scenes.

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:16.070
Staging is a choice of
location for a scene,

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:18.900
while blocking is the
movement within the space.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:20.980
In the moment we mentioned earlier,

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:24.293
her choice of staging adds new
meaning to a classic scene.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:27.482
Jo and Laurie dancing wildly at the ball

00:06:27.482 --> 00:06:30.513
is a classic scene from
Louisa May Alcott's novel.

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:32.840
Laurie: I'll tell you how we could manage.

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:33.970
Laurie: I have an idea.

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:35.489
Laurie: I have an idea
of how we could manage.

00:06:35.489 --> 00:06:37.470
Narrator: In the book,
Jo explains to Laurie

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:38.640
that she must stay hidden

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:40.989
because she scorched
the back of her dress.

00:06:40.989 --> 00:06:42.571
So, to bend the rules,

00:06:42.571 --> 00:06:44.537
Jo and Laurie dance in the hallway.

00:06:44.537 --> 00:06:47.120
Gerwig decides to go in
a different direction

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:50.210
and stages the scene outside
of the Gardeners' house.

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:51.690
Gerwig told The New York Times

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:53.919
that she chose to stage it in this way

00:06:53.919 --> 00:06:55.650
because you could see the dancers inside

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:59.030
and Laurie and Jo having
their own dance party outside.

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:01.040
What Gerwig accomplishes with this staging

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:03.090
is the idea of parallel reality.

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:05.115
Jo and Laurie move in between the windows

00:07:05.115 --> 00:07:06.820
with faux elegance

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:08.430
and then become who they really are

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:09.986
in the space between.

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:13.640
We learn how they see the world

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:16.460
and how they view themselves
as something different.

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:19.503
In contrast, in the 1994 adaptation,

00:07:19.503 --> 00:07:20.812
the scene cuts back and forth

00:07:20.812 --> 00:07:23.570
from Christian Bale and
Winona Ryder to the ballroom

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:25.635
in order to show this juxtaposition.

00:07:25.635 --> 00:07:28.420
This is a classic film
technique to show contrast.

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:30.472
Gerwig's restaging adds a new layer

00:07:30.472 --> 00:07:32.661
of excitement and visual metaphor.

00:07:32.661 --> 00:07:35.278
Another example of excellent
staging in the film

00:07:35.278 --> 00:07:37.320
occurs when Jo and Beth are on the beach.

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:39.500
This is perhaps the best use of Gerwig's

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:41.040
place juxtaposition.

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:42.898
Throughout the film, she navigates

00:07:42.898 --> 00:07:44.610
between past and present,

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:46.430
often anchored by place.

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:48.483
This is a great storytelling technique

00:07:48.483 --> 00:07:50.083
in the screenplay, but the staging

00:07:50.083 --> 00:07:51.870
of the two beach scenes in particular

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:53.670
shows excellent directing.

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:55.920
The past is lit by a warm glow.

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:57.502
In the present it's cloudy,

00:07:57.502 --> 00:08:00.070
as if the joy has gone
out of the atmosphere.

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:02.800
The wind pulls in the
tide with a cloud of sand.

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:04.400
Jo tries to hold Beth tight,

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:05.890
but she can't save her.

00:08:05.890 --> 00:08:08.162
The dramatic staging of
this moment shows that

00:08:08.162 --> 00:08:11.733
for all of Jo's spirit, she
can't stop the inevitable.

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:14.682
Passing over Gerwig for this nomination

00:08:14.682 --> 00:08:16.231
perfectly illustrates the reason

00:08:16.231 --> 00:08:18.820
why this film is so important.

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:21.250
It's easy to see how
scenes full of violence,

00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:24.620
explosions, and life-or-death
drama feel important.

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:27.840
Traditionally, these
themes are more masculine.

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:30.200
It takes very special
director to create a film

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:32.770
about domestic affairs
without being tossed aside

00:08:32.770 --> 00:08:34.014
as a "chick flick."

00:08:34.014 --> 00:08:35.752
"Little Women" is important because it's

00:08:35.752 --> 00:08:38.600
an adaptation about the
place of women in the world

00:08:38.600 --> 00:08:41.100
and how their passions outside
of romantic relationships

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:42.540
are often overlooked.

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:45.100
The distinctive style
and flair of the film

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:47.767
is something only Gerwig could achieve.

